    <string name="agricultural_safety_title">Important Agricultural Safety</string>
    <string name="agricultural_safety_body">Weather-based guidance is calculated for general agricultural awareness and does NOT guarantee spraying safety. Pesticide efficacy and rainfast periods vary by chemical formulation. Always read the product label, follow recommended dosages, wear proper protective equipment (PPE), and adhere to local agricultural extension guidelines. Never rely solely on weather estimates for chemical dosages.</string>

    <!-- Spraying Explanations -->
    <string name="exp_optimal">Weather conditions are favorable for spraying. Temperature, wind, and humidity are within recommended agricultural thresholds with minimal rain risk.</string>
    <string name="exp_high_wind">Strong winds or gusts detected. Spraying during high winds causes chemical drift, uneven coverage, and risks contaminating neighboring crops.</string>
    <string name="exp_calm_wind">Wind is very calm (&lt;3 km/h). Extreme calm may indicate a temperature inversion, causing spray particles to float and drift off-target.</string>
    <string name="exp_high_temp">High temperatures detected (&gt;32°C). High heat causes rapid evaporation of spray droplets and risks leaf scorching (phytotoxicity).</string>
    <string name="exp_low_temp">Low temperatures detected (&lt;10°C). Cold temperatures can reduce pesticide efficacy and systemic absorption.</string>
    <string name="exp_rain_expected">Rain or high precipitation probability expected. Rain can wash off sprayed chemicals before they dry or absorb.</string>
    <string name="exp_high_humidity">High humidity (&gt;85%) slows droplet drying and may promote fungal infections if foliage remains wet.</string>
    <string name="exp_low_humidity">Low humidity (&lt;40%) accelerates droplet evaporation before reaching target foliage.</string>
    <string name="exp_insufficient_data">Unable to evaluate spraying conditions due to unavailable weather data.</string>

